# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# One entry, and it is here because the report it silences is correct.
#
# A seqlock writes the value bytes while readers are reading them, with no
# atomic on those bytes. The sequence counter and the fences around it are what
# make a reader able to detect that it raced and retry, and they do not make the
# accesses themselves non-racy. The C++ memory model defines the race on the
# accesses, so ThreadSanitizer is right by the letter of the standard and the
# technique is a data race by that definition.
#
# The long version, including what the standard-clean alternative costs and why
# Linux's own seqlock_t does the same thing, is at the top of
# include/tick/seqlock.hpp. Read that before deciding this suppression is a
# shortcut.
#
# What is NOT suppressed, deliberately:
#   - anything in MpmcQueue, which runs clean and must stay that way
#   - anything on the seqlock's sequence counter or its fences, which would be
#     a real bug rather than the known one
#
# The tests still assert the property that matters, which is that no reader ever
# accepts a value assembled from two different writes. That assertion has never
# failed in any run, suppressed or not.
race:tick::SeqLock
